1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pete was born to a swedish mother and a Norwegian father.  Family history hypothesized they may have been a matched through a "mail order" bride service.  At age 2, his mother  been widowed while pregnant with her 
2 CONC second child, Theoline.  His father, Thomas, had fallen off a ladder at the construction site he was working on - designing and building a church with a classic Scandinavian steeple - like the church had at his native home of Fla.
2 CONC    His grave was one of the first in the new church's cemetery.  Maria Kajsa's father returned from Sweden to broker her another husband and father for the family.  2 years later, Maria Kajsa was married to Ole E Olsen, a Norwegia
2 CONC n farmer with a farm across the street from the cemetery.  After years of bad luck in farming and a bad economy, the Olsens moved to Sawyer co wisconsin, drawn by cheap land.  Pete had worked several farms and even gone to Manitob
2 CONC a looking for a job and a home.  He and his sister, Theoline ended up in Sawyer Co and worked as a lumberjack for a french canadian named  Joe Buckwheat.   One of Joe's daughters caught his fancy -  15 year old Tessie.  They marri
2 CONC ed in 1918 shortly before Pete was shipped overseas w/ the US army 164th AM-TRAN support unit.  He learned how to shoot, drive, and repair all manner of equiptment.  He was only in France for a couple of weeks before the end of th
2 CONC e war.  No one left living knows the story of his exposure to mustard gas - but when Pete died, the army sent his widow, Tessie a large life insurance settlement, according to Les Roppe, his oldest son.  They were also plagued wit
2 CONC h the loss of 3 chlldren shortly after birth.  Orville "Buck" Roppe was the only other child to survive.  Pete attempted to farm, but made most of his money fixing auto's for his friends and a car dealer in Park Falls.  His son Le
2 CONC s remembers the Hupmobile his parents owned fondly.  HE also remembers the day his dad bled to death in their home - "there was blood everywhere - a lot of blood.  His mom didn't get there in time to say goodbye and never forgave 
2 CONC mom for it."  In 1932 the farm house and barn burned to the ground, almost killing the family - they lost everything.  It was found to be due to faulty wiring, but Tess and the boys took the insurance money and moved to Cameron, W
2 CONC isconsin - 45 miles away.  She opened a restauraunt/bar called Tessie's place.  Pete and Tessie are buried at Winter Cemetery, with the 3 babies they lost nearby in unmarked graves.  
