1 NAME Lisbeth Andersdatter /Klyne/
2 GIVN Lisbeth Andersdatter
2 SURN Klyne
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 DEC 1585
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ribe
3 STAE Syddanmark
3 CTRY Danmark
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JUN 1659
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ribe
3 STAE Syddanmark
3 CTRY Danmark
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} J. Kinck Ribe bys historie bind 2. p. 222
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Lisbeth''' ægtede 1604''' Jens Andersen (Guldsmed),''' af en anset Familie i Ribe. Han var Kjøbmand og drev navnlig Øxenhandel; men det synes, som om den selvforskyldte Ulykke skulde forfølge And. Sørensens Slægt til det Yderst
2 CONC e; thi 1625 indtraf den skrækkelige Begivenhed, at han under en Strid dræbte sin egen Broder,''' Haus Andersen.''' Om denne Begivenhed og dens Følger vil blive fortalt nærmere ved det næste Ar.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jens Andersen Guldsmed, født 1570 i Guldsmedehjemmet, Ribe, stilling drev Studehandel på Holland, død ca. 1647. Jens Andersen Guldsmed og Lisbeth AndersdatterKlyne, der boede i et hus på Nederdammen i Ribe, drev Studehandel på Hol
2 CONC land. Imidlertidindtraf der 31. juli 1625 en sørgelig begivenhed for familien, idet Jens Andersen Guldsmeddræbte sin broder Hans Andersen, der var kaptajn ved landfolket. Herom fortælles i J. Kinchs "Ribe Bys Historie" følgende: "
2 CONC Den næstældste Broder, Raadmand Søren Andersen Guldsmed, ejede en Have udenfor Nørreport, rimeligvis et offentligt forlystelssted, hvor der fandtes en "Pilkentavl", på hvilken der spilledes med brikker. Den nævnte dag om eftermidd
2 CONC agen vare Hans og Jens Andersen Guldsmed tilligemed nogle andre borgere af den mere formuende klasse,rådmand Knud Lambertsen, Jens Jonsen Trellund, Daniel Iversen Vandel og et par til, samlede i denne have spillende på "Pilkentavl
2 CONC en" og drak vin. De to brødre blev uenige om spillet, og Hans bliv hidsig. Jens ville da gå, men vendte ulykkeligvis om for at betale for vinen. Hans gav påny mange onde ord, og Jens hentydede da til noget vedkommende deres fædren
2 CONC earv, som endnu ikke var skiftet imellem dem. Herover blev Hans så forbitret, at han drog sin kniv og angreb broderen. Knud Lambertsen søgte forgæves at holde ham; han rev sig løs og løb efter Jens, som imidlertid var kommen op i 
2 CONC gården og nu ligeledes drog sine knivefrem for at forsvare sig. Jens må da også være bleven hidsig og gav sin broder et stik i brystet, hvoraf han døde, som det synes, meget hurtigt; thi det omtales ikke, at han har afgivet nogen 
2 CONC forklaring før sin død. Alle de øvrige tilstedeværendes vidnesbyrd vare gunstige for Jens, hvorfor tovsmændene den 10de Septbr. afsagde deres ed således, at drabet erklæredes for nødværge, og Jens Andersen altså svores til sin fre
2 CONC d. Herskabsfogden, Mads Lassen Lime, fandt sig dog ikke tilfredsstillet ved denne ed, men indstævnede den for borgmester og råd. Rimeligvis har lensmanden, Hr.Albr. Skeel fra begyndelsen af stået bagved ham, måske tildeles fordi H
2 CONC ans Andersen havde været i kongens tjeneste, medens borgerskabet vistnok hele tiden har ønsket, at Jens Andersen skulle slippe så let som muligt. Sagen blev trukken ud, og imidlertid tilbød Jens Andersen til tinge at udlægge den l
2 CONC ovlige mandebod, hvis nogen ville modtage den, og udsonede sig med sin svigerinde, som med sin broder, Rasmus Madsen, borger i Varde, og Søren Andersen Guldsmed som børnenes lavværge den 11te Januar 1626 udstedte et orfejdebrev fo
2 CONC r ham, hvorefter han tillige søgte et kongeligt lejdebrev. Et sådant fik han dog ikke; tværtimod udgik der en kongelig befaling til lensmanden på Riberhus om at undersøge, om de vidnesbyrd, til hvilke sandemandstovet (som det her 
2 CONC kaldes) støttede sig, vare rigtige. Lensmandens beretning må have stadfæstet den tvivl, som man i forvejen nærede, og den 29e Januar 1626 udgik der et nyt kongebrev til ham, hvorved det pålagdes ham at befordre sagen tildoms og fø
2 CONC re tilsyn med, at begge partier ikke sker andet end ret. Den 24de februar foretoges sagen på rådhuset. I almindelighed vare borgm. og råd ikke meget bøjelige til at dømme imod en af lensmanden eller endogså af regjeringen udtalt a
2 CONC nskuelse, uagtet der tilvisse findes eksempler derpå (f.eks. i sagen angående Anders Sørensen Klyns drab og i et par trolddomssager). Herskabsfogeden formente nu i dennesag, at der ikke burde være tale om nødværge, for det første,
2 CONC  fordi Jens kunne være gået sinvej, medens han i dets sted var vendt tilbage og selv havde tirret sin broder, for det andet, fordi det ikke kunne antages, at Hans havde haft den hensigt at dræbe Jens, eftersom han ivirkeligheden s
2 CONC let ingen skade havde tilføjet ham, hvilket dog ville have været let for ham, især da Jens, efter at have taget sine knive frem, først tabte dem i græsset, så at han måtte bukke sig for at tage dem op. - Retten kom til det samme r
2 CONC esultat; den fandt, at Jens kunne være undvegen, hvis han havde villet; den vidste derfor ikke "Tovsmændenes Ed ved Magt at kunne kjende, ej heller Jens Andersen sin Fred at kunne nyde". Det var en alvorlig sag for sandemænd, nævn
2 CONC inger eller tovmænd, når de fældedes for en af dem afsagt ed, da de ifølge Kristian d. 3dies Reces af 1558, §27 derved forbrøde 3 Mk. til den, som fældede dem, og deres Boslod til deres Herskab, i Kjøbstederne vistnok halvt til Ko
2 CONC ngen, halvt til Byen. For flere Kjøbsteders Vedkommende var der rigtignok bestemmelser, Page 17
2 CONT
2 CONT hvorved kongen havde tilstået formildelser i denne strenge ret; men for Ribe by kjendes ingen sådan. Derimod kunne der nok tinges med lensmanden og byen, og det måtte tovsmændene i denne sag også gjøre; men herunder kom det frem, 
2 CONC at Mag. Kristen Friis, den theologiske Læsemester i Ribe, havde spillet en vigtig Rolle i denne sag. Han varen fætter til Jens og Hans Andersen, idet deres mødre vare søstre. Desuden var han gift medIngeborg Lauridsdatter, en most
2 CONC er til Jens Andersens hustru. Om ham oplystes det nu, at han havde indvirket stærkt på tovsmændene for at få dem til at gøre deres ed således, som de gjorde den; ja, da en af dem, Villum Hansen, havde betænkeligheder ved sagen, ha
2 CONC vde magisteren endogså givet ham en skriftlig forsikring om, at han ville indestå ham for den skade, som han derved kunne komme i. Sagen får herved et mindre godt udseende, end når man bedømmer den alene efter de under sagen fremk
2 CONC omne vidnesbyrd. Da regjeringen fik underretning herom, udgik der den 30te maj samme år et kongebrev til lensmanden om, at magisterens brev skulle være ugyldigt, eftersom detvar utilbørligt, at den, som selv havde gjort eden, skul
2 CONC le gå fr, men at lensmanden skulle aftinge dem begge for deres del i sagen. Kristen Friis blev som den rigeste, den som måtte bøde mest; thi af Herskabsfogdens regnskab over sagefald i regnskabsåret 1629-30 ses, at han for denne s
2 CONC ag måtte betale kongen 75 Rdl., medens Tovmændene slap med at betale fra3 til 20 Rdl., rimeligvis efter hver enkelts formue; 3 af dem slap fri eller betalteidetmindste ikke det år. Ligeså meget, som hver enkelt betalte kongen, måt
2 CONC te han rimeligvis også betale byen. Jens Andersen Guldsmed var under sagens gang flygtet bort, måske straks efter drabet. Den 23de Marts 1626 skænkede kongen hans enke og børn hans boslod, som ifølge den ham overgåede dom var tilf
2 CONC alden kongen. Efter en gammel optagnelse levedemanden indtil sin død (senest 1647) hos sin broder, Hr. Kristen i Højerup. Rigtignok skulle efter Kristian den 3dies Reces af 1536, §7 Ingen, som var dømt fredløs for et drab i konger
2 CONC iget, kunde "huses, hæles eller have nogen plads" i hertugdømmerne; men det kom naturligvis meget an på, om der var nogen af den afdødes slægt, som ville efterstræbe drabsmanden, og det var der jo i dette tilfælde ikke. - Hans hus
2 CONC tru blev derimod boende i Ribe, hvor hun havde ret anselige ejendomme, indtil hun døde i pestens tid. Han blev gift med 8 jul 1604 i Ribe kirke & sogn, Lisbeth Andersdatter Klyn
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 13/3 1626. Peder Pedersen, borger her, på vegne af Lisbeth, Jens Andersens, lavbød hende og hendes husbonds huse, stalde og ejendom.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14/3 1626. Hans Friis, borger her, på vegne af Jens Andersen og hans hustru Lisbeth Andersdatter, sælger til dr. Christen Borum tvende søsterlodder i det hus, som Christen Borum ibor, beliggende i Stenbrogade. Den ene søsterlod ar
2 CONC vet efter hans hustrus afd. bror Søren Andersen, og den anden lod, som han har købt af Jens Klyne, som han var tilfalden efter afd. Søren Andersen.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2/12 1633. Niels Pedersen, på vegne af Lisbeth Andersdatter, født i Ribe, opsagde arv og gæld efter hendes afd. far Anders Sørensen og hendes mor MarineAnderskone, som nu er i hospitalet på grund af armod.
2 CONT
2 CONT 18/7 1639. Peder Pedersen byfoged, på vegne af Lisbeth, Jens Andersens, og Anna, mester Peders, afhændede fra mester Frederich Andersen Klyne til Lisbeth, Jens Andersens, mester Frederich A. Klynes lod i agre i Karup?, og på Peder
2 CONC  Jacobsens vegne til Anne, afd. mester Peder Hegelunds hans og hans hustrus arvepart i agre.
2 CONT
2 CONT 18/7 1639. Alle afd. Kirsten, Søren Andersens, efterlevendes rette arvinger inclusive Anne, afd. Peder Hegelunds søn mester Laurits Pedersen Hegelund og Lisbeths, Jens Andersens, søn Anders Jensen og Hans Jensen den unge, på egne 
2 CONC og medsøskendes vegne, giver Peder Pedersen fuldmagt at sælge til Hans Nielsen Friis 20 Øffuers? agre.???
2 CONT
2 CONT 5/10 1639. Mads Lassen Lime rådm., på vegne af Lisbeth, afd. Jens Andersens, solgte til Anders Jensen guldsmed, hendes hus i Storegade mellem Peder Jacobsens, borger i Odense, hus på østerside og møllestrømmen på den vesterside.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10/9 1642. Peder Jacobsen, borger i Odense, g.m. Maren, datter af rådm.30 A.S.? Klyne, giver sin svoger Anders Jensen Guldsmed, det vil sige søn af Marens søster Lisbeth, fuldmagt til at sælge Peder Jacobsens hus i Storegade (mell
2 CONC em Anders J. Guldsmeds hus og Peder Rellesens? hus på vester side) til Niels Jacobsen Bonum? Brunn?
2 CONT
2 CONT 66 22/5 1647. Lisbett Andersdatter, afd. Jens Andersens, i hendes søn Anders Jensen guldsmeds nærværelse, solgte til Hans Friis et hus i Sortebrødregade.
2 CONT
2 CONT 53b (1654) Anders Jensen guldsmed, på sin mor Lisbeth Andersdatters, afd. Jens Andersens, vegne solgte til hans søster Kirsten Jensdatter et hus i Sortebrødregade. Lisbeth Andersdatter skal have fri hus der sin levetid.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5/10 1639. Mads Lassen Lime rådm., på vegne af Lisbeth, afd. Jens Andersens, solgte til Anders Jensen guldsmed, hendes hus i Storegade mellem Peder Jacobsens, borger i Odense, hus på østerside og møllestrømmen på den vesterside.
2 CONT
2 CONT 61 18/10 1641. Anders Jensen guldsmed, med fuldmagt fra Anders Pedersen Hegelund? og hr. Jens Pedersen Hegelund?, solgte til M.L.H. [Mester Laurits Hegelund?] deres del i deres mors hus.
2 CONT
2 CONT 10/9 1642. Peder Jacobsen, borger i Odense, g.m. Maren, datter af rådm.30 A.S.? Klyne, giver sin svoger Anders Jensen Guldsmed, det vil sige søn af Marens søster Lisbeth, fuldmagt til at sælge Peder Jacobsens hus i Storegade (mell
2 CONC em Anders J. Guldsmeds hus og Peder Rellesens? hus på vester side) til Niels Jacobsen Bonum? Brunn?
2 CONT
2 CONT 66 22/5 1647. Lisbett Andersdatter, afd. Jens Andersens, i hendes søn Anders Jensen guldsmeds nærværelse, solgte til Hans Friis et hus i Sortebrødregade.
2 CONT
2 CONT 53b (1654) Anders Jensen guldsmed, på sin mor Lisbeth Andersdatters, afd. Jens Andersens, vegne solgte til hans søster Kirsten Jensdatter et hus i Sortebrødregade. Lisbeth Andersdatter skal have fri hus der sin levetid.
2 CONT
2 CONT 53b (1654) Anders Jensen guldsmed, på sin mor Lisbeth Andersdatters, afd. Jens Andersens, vegne solgte til hans søster Kirsten Jensdatter et hus i Sortebrødregade. Lisbeth Andersdatter skal have fri hus der sin levetid.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.brejl.dk/ribeting.html
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ***
2 CONT
2 CONT I, Kitrina Jackson, direct descendant (she is my 10th  great Grandmother) so I have translated some of the above to English as follows: 
2 CONT
2 CONT J. Kinck Ribe town history volume 2. p. 222
2 CONT
2 CONT Lisbeth married Jens Andersen (Goldsmith) in 1604, from a respected family in Ribe. He was a merchant and mainly dealt in the axe trade; but it seems as if the self-inflicted misfortune would haunt And. Sørensen's family to the ut
2 CONC most; for in 1625 the terrible event occurred that he killed his own brother, Haus Andersen, during a fight. This event and its consequences will be told in more detail next year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jens Andersen Guldsmed, born 1570 in Guldsmedehjemmet, Ribe, position ran Studehandel in Holland, died ca. 1647. Jens Andersen Guldsmed and Lisbeth AndersdatterKlyne, who lived in a house on Nederdammen in Ribe, ran Studehandel in
2 CONC  Holland. However, on 31 July 1625 a sad event occurred for the family, as Jens Andersen Guldsmed killed his brother Hans Andersen, who was a captain with the peasants. The following is told about this in J. Kinch's "History of Ri
2 CONC be Town": "The second oldest brother, Councilman Søren Andersen Guldsmed, owned a garden outside Nørreport, probably a public place of amusement, where there was a "Pilkentavl" on which people played with checkers.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the afternoon of the day mentioned, Hans and Jens Andersen Guldsmed, along with some other citizens of the wealthier class, alderman Knud Lambertsen, Jens Jonsen Trellund, Daniel Iversen Vandel and a couple more, gathered in th
2 CONC is garden playing on the "Pilkentavlen" and drinking wine. The two brothers disagreed about the game, and Hans became angry. Jens wanted to leave, but unfortunately turned around to pay for the wine. Hans again said many bad words
2 CONC , and Jens then alluded to something concerning their ancestral inheritance, which had not yet been divided between them. Hans became so embittered at this that he drew his knife and attacked his brother. Knud Lambertsen tried in 
2 CONC vain to hold him; he broke free and ran after Jens, who had meanwhile come up into the yard and now also drew his knives to defend himself. Jens must also have become angry and stabbed his brother in the chest, from which he died,
2 CONC  as it seems, very quickly; for it is not mentioned that he gave any explanation before his death. All the other witnesses' testimonies were favorable to Jens, which is why the ropemen on September 10th took their oath so that the
2 CONC  killing was declared self-defense, and Jens Andersen was thus sworn to his peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bailiff, Mads Lassen Lime, was not satisfied with this oath, but summoned the mayor and council. It is reasonable that the sheriff, Mr. Albr. Skeel, has been behind him from the beginning, perhaps because Hans Andersen had bee
2 CONC n in the king's service, while the bourgeoisie has probably always wanted Jens Andersen to get off as easily as possible. The case was dragged out, and in the meantime Jens Andersen offered to the Thing to pay the legal fine if an
2 CONC yone wanted to receive it, and reconciled with his sister-in-law, who, together with her brother, Rasmus Madsen, citizen of Varde, and Søren Andersen Guldsmed as the children's guardian, issued a letter of fief for him on 11 Janua
2 CONC ry 1626, after which he also sought a royal letter of lease. However, he did not receive such a letter; on the contrary, a royal order was issued to the sheriff of Riberhus to investigate whether the testimonies on which the sandm
2 CONC en's lodge (as it is called here) relied were correct. The sheriff's report must have confirmed the doubts that were already held, and on 29 January 1626 a new royal letter was issued to him, ordering him to bring the case to tria
2 CONC l and to see that both parties were treated fairly. On 24 February the case was heard at the town hall. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In general, the mayor and council were not very flexible in ruling against an opinion expressed by the sheriff or even by the government, although there are certainly examples of this (e.g. in the case concerning the murder of And
2 CONC ers Sørensen Klyn and in a couple of witchcraft cases). The bailiff now believed in this case that there should be no question of self-defense, firstly because Jens could have gone his way, while instead he had returned and himsel
2 CONC f had teased his brother, secondly because it could not be assumed that Hans had intended to kill Jens, since in reality he had done him no harm at all, which would have been easy for him, especially since Jens, after taking out h
2 CONC is knives, first dropped them in the grass, so that he had to bend down to pick them up. - The court came to the same result; it found that Jens could have escaped if he had wanted to; it therefore did not know "The Ropemen's " To
2 CONC  be able to know the oath by force, nor to be able to enjoy Jens Andersen's peace". It was a serious matter for sandmen, jurors or tovmen when they were convicted for one of them having taken an oath, since according to Kristian d
2 CONC . 3dies Reces of 1558, §27, they thereby forfeited 3 Mk. to the one who convicted them, and their Boslod to their lordship, in the places of purchase probably half to the king, half to the city. For the case of several places of p
2 CONC urchase there were indeed provisions, Page 17
2 CONT
2 CONT whereby the king had granted leniencies in this strict law; but for the town of Ribe none such is known. On the other hand, it was possible to make a deal with the sheriff and the town, and the tovsmends in this case had to do the
2 CONC  same; but it emerged that Mag. Kristen Friis, the theological reading master in Ribe, had played an important role in this case. He was a cousin of Jens and Hans Andersen, as their mothers were sisters. Furthermore, he was marrie
2 CONC d to Ingeborg Lauridsdatter, an aunt of Jens Andersen's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was now reported about him that he had exerted great influence on the ropers to make them take their oath as they did; indeed, when one of them, Villum Hansen, had misgivings about the matter, the magistrate had even given him 
2 CONC a written assurance that he would guarantee him for any damage he might suffer as a result. The case thus takes on a less favorable appearance than when judged solely by the testimonies given during the case. When the government w
2 CONC as informed of this, a royal letter was issued on 30 May of the same year to the sheriff stating that the magistrate's letter should be invalid, since it was improper for the one who had taken the oath to go ahead, but that the sh
2 CONC eriff should settle both of them for their part in the case. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristen Friis, as the richest, was the one who had to pay the most fine; for from the Herskabsfogden's account of cases in the fiscal year 1629-30 it is seen that for this case he had to pay the king 75 Rdl., while the Tovmændene 
2 CONC got away with paying from 3 to 20 Rdl., reasonably according to each individual's wealth; 3 of them got away with it or did not pay the property at least that year. As much as each individual paid the king, he also had to reasonab
2 CONC ly pay the city. Jens Andersen Guldsmed had fled during the course of the case, perhaps immediately after the murder. On 23 March 1626 the king granted his widow and children his estate, which according to the judgment passed on h
2 CONC im had accrued to the king. According to an old record, the man lived until his death (at the latest 1647) with his brother, Mr. Kristen in Højerup. Indeed, according to Christian the 3rd's Reces of 1536, §7, no one who had been c
2 CONC onvicted of murder in the kingdom could "be housed, kept or have any place" in the duchies; but it naturally depended greatly on whether there were any relatives of the deceased, who wanted to pursue the murderer, and in this case
2 CONC  there was none. - His wife, on the other hand, remained living in Ribe, where she had quite considerable property, until she died during the plague. He married on 8 July 1604 in Ribe church & parish, Lisbeth Andersdatter Klyn
