2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sognepræst i Starup-Grarup. (Johannes Ancharius), immatrikuleret i Rostock og tog magistergrad i 1552. Hjælpepræst i Starup-Grarup fra 1554 og Sognepræst samme sogn fra 1574 til sin død. I kirken er et monument over magister Johan
2 CONC nes Ancharius, præst ved Starup og Grarup kirker og hans hustru Rachel, ( Iver Corvini (d.e.Ravns) datter.)::
2 CONT  " Men de ombyttede det jordiske med det himmelske liv, han 20.jan.1602 i hans alders 75.år,
2 CONT    hun 17. sept.1611, alder 78 år."
2 CONT
2 CONT (Ref: Anne-Marie Mejer (Baggesen)).
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT [89] Hans Anchersen (also known by the latin influenced name: Johannes Ancharius) took matriculation in Rostock, he would later get his"magistergrad" in 1552. Was assistant pastor (hjælpepræst) for Starup-Grarup parish from 1554, 
2 CONC and would become pastor (sognepræst)there from 1574 until his death. Starup is located just a few kilometres south-east of Haderslev, and Grarup is just a few kilometers further east of that. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the history of the Duchies of Schleswig and Holstein, and their relationship to Danmark:  Until sometime under King Valdemar II (reigned 1202-41), the Kingdom of Danmark bordered onto the germanic states just south of the Slien
2 CONC  (Schlei) inlet. Valdemar II awarded his second son the southern part of Jylland as a duchy - from the Slien in the south (in between the towns of Schleswig and Kiel) north up the Jylland peninsula to the river Konge-Aa, however t
2 CONC he northern border of the duchy followed only the Konge-Aa until just east of Ribe, then it drops south, then west towards the coast. While Schleswig was given the status of a duchy, its Duke ultimately owed fealty to the Danish k
2 CONC ing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchy of Schleswig has traditionally reached from the rivers Eiderand the Slien, in the south, to Konge-Aa, in the north. The Duchy of Holstein from the river Eider, in the north, to the river Elbe, in the south. Later, inheri
2 CONC tance rights for Schleswig became a matter of contention. Gradually some areas were considered Royal domain of Danmark and some of Prussia, other parts were ruled by the Duke of Gottorp, and some parts were ruled in common. In the
2 CONC  Duchy of Schleswig some cities had independent status (known as imperial free-cities), ruled directly by the Kingdom of Danmark. The Duchy of Holstein was split up in thismanner as well: parts were considered royal domain of the 
2 CONC Danmark; partsthe domain of the Duke of Gottorp, some common areas, some ruled by the germans (Prussia). This situation was untenable to the rulers involved, and eventually led to fighting. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1658 the Duke of Gottorp-Holstein became sovereign over the "Duke'spart" of the Duchies Schleswig and Holstein, however it never was a "territorial entity" and it was a constant reason for quarrels and wars. Very simplistically
2 CONC  the Duchy of Schleswig became totally "royal"(Danish) in 1721, and the Duke of Gottorp was restricted to the Holsteinareas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1806, as a result of the "Napoleonic reorganisation", Schleswig and Holstein came under the control of the Danish king (with an independent administration). Danmark had sided with Napolean against England afterrefusing to hand 
2 CONC over its naval fleet to England. In 1807 the Engish navy blockaded, and then bombarded København, which destroyed the city and killed thousands of its citizens. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1815 the Congress of Vienna ruled the Duchy of Holstein ("south of the Eider") to be a part of the "German Bund" (i.e. all the Germanic states and Austria). In 1846 the Danish King ruled Schleswig to be part of his kingdom, and
2 CONC  that the heritage rights of the Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Augustenburg was restricted to Holstein. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1848 both Duchies had declared themselves independant, and on April 4,1848, Schleswig was accepted into the German Bund. However on May 8,1852, under the pressure of the "European powers", the London Treaty gave both duchies ba
2 CONC ck to Danmark. Schleswig received a constitution on February 15, 1854, and Holstein on June 11, 1854. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of King Frederick VII, in 1863, ended the heritage rights of the Danish kings to the Duchies; and the Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Augustenburg tried to gain power. The German Bund raised war against Danmark, wh
2 CONC ich on October 30, 1864 was forced to hand the Duchies (less Lauenburg) over to the German Bund. From 1864-66 the Duchies were ruled by Austria-Prussia. After the Prussian/Austrian war in 1866 they were made a Prussian province. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1864 the border at Konge-Aa was established until 1920. After which a referendum was held, the people in the northern half of Schleswig voted to become part of Danmark; the people in the southern half to join Germany. Thus Schl
2 CONC eswig remains split in half to this very day. 
2 CONT
