1 NAME Elva Petrine /Svenningsen/
2 GIVN Elva Petrine
2 SURN Svenningsen
2 _MARNM deCatanzaro
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Yarmouth
3 STAE Nova Scotia
3 CTRY Canada
1 BIRT
2 DATE 9 MAR 1877
2 ADDR
3 CITY Aarhus
3 STAE Central Denmark Region
3 CTRY Denmark
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 MAY 1943
2 ADDR
3 CITY Vankleek Hill
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph & Elva lived at 90 Union Street during the early years of their marriage, and later at 94 West 75th Street (now Bay Ridge Parkway) and 2521 Avenue M in Brooklyn. Elva was one child in a large Danish family, 
2 CONC and while her given names was Elvine, she clearly believed (similar to her husband) that it was necessary to revert to an anglicized equivalent as a means of assimilating into American society in order to sound less foreign. While
2 CONC  Elva often boasted that she was raised in a military family to a highly regarded Danish colonel, her father actually lost his left hand in the Second Schleswig War of 1864 (when he was only 22) and for the remainder of his life w
2 CONC orked as a painting assistant. Elva’s initial record of immigration aboard the SS Circassia in 1893 lists her as a servant. Her marriage to Joseph did not appear to be a happy one, and Elva was known to have had many affairs. An a
2 CONC rticle from the New York Times dated 4 Sep 1921 indicates that she the victim of a lawsuit for $100,000 set by Mrs. Louise Auditore for  “the alleged alienations of the affections of her husband”, Frank Auditore, a wealthy stevedo
2 CONC re in business with Joseph. Elva was subsequently arrested and released on $5,000 bail set by her husband. While she was known to visit her family in Europe on an annual basis spending little time at home with her husband, their s
2 CONC eparation became permanent in 1923 when Elva purchased a home in Yarmouth, Nova Scotia to be near her mother and sister Margrethe, gaining permanent custody of their son Carmino. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elva is believed to have crossed the Atlantic Ocean over seventy times in her life and passenger listings of the SS Olaf suggest that her annual trips to Denmark continued well into her senior years. These records also make it gla
2 CONC ringly evident that she frequently lied about her age when abroad to present herself as a younger woman, although the usefulness of such a tactic remains highly questionable as photographs from the 1930’s indicate that a weakening
2 CONC  heart condition had given her such a weathered face as to make her look at least 10 years her senior. Her own son never knew the truth about her age, believing her to have been born in 1879. Only after her husband’s death did she
2 CONC  begin posing as an Italian Countess, while somehow persuading her son to adopt a “de” prefix to their surname no earlier than 1941. Elva died of a heart attack in the spring of 1943 while visiting her son who had recently been ap
2 CONC pointed as the country parish bishop at Vankleek Hill. Carmino had her remains taken back to Yarmouth for burial, likely at Mountain West Cemetery where her sister and mother are also buried.
1 NOTE {geni:height_1} 5 feet
1 NOTE {geni:height_2} 5 inches
